

:: day_2_1
<img src="img/locations/valdmere/broken_antler_room.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />
Sleep didn't come. Not properly. I drifted in and out of a shallow, restless half-consciousness, the kind where your body lies still but your brain runs laps around a dark track, reviewing every contingency, every exit strategy, every way tonight could go sideways and spill you into the dirt.
<<removeobjective "day_1">>
<img src="img/scene/day_2/1.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />
<<addobjective "day_2" "Wake up and prepare for the mine job">>
The room was dark. Rain had stopped an hour ago   I'd heard it taper off, the hammering on the shutters fading to a patter and then to silence, the silence somehow louder than the storm. Through the window, the sky was the deep indigo of pre-dawn, starless, the clouds still thick but emptied.

[[Wake Up|day_2_2]]

:: day_2_2
<img src="img/locations/valdmere/broken_antler_room.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Mira was awake beside me. I knew because her breathing had changed   the slow, measured rhythm of sleep replaced by the slightly faster, slightly shallower pattern of someone lying still with their eyes open, thinking hard. She hadn't moved. Neither had I. Two professional liars, pretending to sleep for each other's benefit.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You're awake.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm resting my eyes.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Your eyes are open. I can see them glowing.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Half-elf eyes don't glow. They reflect ambient light. There's a difference.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
They're glowing, Mira.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Fine. I'm awake. I've been awake for twenty minutes, running scenarios in my head. The junction at fifty paces is the choke point. If they come from both sides, we're done. We need to hit the storage shaft, grab what we can, and be out before the warband mobilizes.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Good morning to you too.
<</dialog>>

She sat up. In the blue-dark of the room, she was all angles and shadow   the sharp line of her jaw, the pale amber of her eyes catching the faint pre-dawn glow from the window, the cropped copper hair sticking up on one side in the way I'd grown unreasonably fond of. She reached under the pillow and pulled out an arrowhead   she slept with one there, always, the way another woman might keep a prayer book or a lucky charm.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Get up. We need to check gear and eat before we go.
<</dialog>>

I swung my legs out of bed. My body was the same body it had always been   heavy, reliable, the thick-muscled frame of a man who'd been swinging steel since his teens. Broad shoulders that bumped the doorframe if I wasn't careful. Chest dark with hair. Big calloused hands that wrapped around a sword hilt with the comfortable familiarity of a handshake. I rolled my neck, felt the vertebrae pop, and scratched the three-day stubble along my jaw.

We ate in the room   cold breakfast from Mira's travel kit. Dried meat, hard bread that was only marginally worse than what the Broken Antler served on purpose, a few strips of salted pork that had seen better weeks. Mira ate with the mechanical efficiency of a soldier fueling up before a march, chewing without tasting, her eyes already somewhere else. I managed half the bread and a few bites of meat before my stomach decided that nerves were more filling than food.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Eat more.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm not hungry.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You're going to be swinging a longsword in a tunnel full of goblins in approximately four hours. Your muscles need fuel. Eat the bread or I'll feed it to you, and I won't be gentle about it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Is that a threat or a promise?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's a nutritional recommendation delivered with appropriate urgency. Eat.
<</dialog>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Eat breakfast and check your gear">>
[[Finish eating bread|day_2_3]]

:: day_2_3

We laid out our gear on Mira's bed   the one that served as our equipment rack, since neither of us used it for sleeping anymore. The inventory was the grim arithmetic of survival.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/2.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Mine: longsword, recently sharpened. Belt knife   short, heavy, good for close work if things got desperate. Leather armor   nothing fancy, hardened cowhide with metal studs at the shoulders and chest, the kind that would stop a glancing blow and not much else. A coil of rope, thirty feet, hemp. A small lantern with three hours of oil, though we'd try to avoid using it   light in a goblin tunnel was an invitation, not an advantage. Flint and steel. Waterskin and five silver from the advance, tucked into my belt pouch. The other five were hidden under the mattress, insurance against the possibility that we didn't come back and someone needed to pay for the room.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Morbid, hiding money under the mattress.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Practical. If we die, the innkeeper finds it, covers our tab, and doesn't sell our gear to recoup. If we live, it's beer money. Either way, the math works.
<</dialog>>

Hers: the longbow, restrung with her spare string, the limbs oiled and supple. Two quivers   twenty-four arrows total, though she'd culled four during yesterday's inspection for imperfect fletching, leaving twenty. The short sword   the cheese-smelling one, freshly sharpened, which she picked up with the expression of a woman lifting a dead rat by the tail. Belt knife. A small leather pouch that she kept in her vest pocket, close to her body: medicinal supplies. Clean cloth strips. A tin of numbing salve. A curved needle and waxed thread for field sutures.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The needle and thread. That's optimistic.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The needle and thread is realistic. One of us is going to get cut. I'd rather stitch it in the field than bleed out discussing the philosophical implications. You ready?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Almost. Run me through the plan one more time.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
In. Find crates. Carry what we can. Out. In that order. Without dying. Ideally.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Short plan.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Best plans are short. Long plans break the moment they meet reality. Short plans bend. We go in quiet, we locate the storage shaft, we grab sealed crates, we leave before anything with teeth notices we were there.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
If something with teeth does notice?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Then we introduce those teeth to our weapons and proceed with the plan. No heroics, Aldric.
<</dialog>>

She said that last part with the flat, deliberate emphasis of someone who'd learned through painful experience that the man she worked with had a definition of "no heroics" that was creative at best and suicidal at worst.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
No heroics. Agreed.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You're saying the words but your face is doing the thing.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What thing?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The thing where your jaw gets that particular set and your eyes go slightly distant and you're already imagining some dramatic last stand in a tunnel with your sword held high and a battle cry on your lips. I've seen it before. In Thornwall. Right before the rat incident.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We agreed never to mention the rat incident.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We agreed YOU would never mention it. I bring it up whenever it serves as a cautionary tale. Which is frequently. No heroics. Say it again and mean it this time.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
No heroics. I mean it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Good. Now put your armor on. We're going shopping.
<</dialog>>
<<set $day_2_4 = false>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Go to the Undercity market and buy supplies for the mine job">>
<<link "Go to the Undercity Market market">>
    <<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>

:: day_2_4
<<set $day_2_4 = true>>
<<set $mcLocation = "undercity">>
The Undercity market operated on a different schedule than the rest of Valdmere. While the merchant quarter opened at dawn with the respectability of a banking institution, the Undercity's stalls and dealers didn't stir until mid-morning, when the clientele   nocturnal by profession or habit   had finished sleeping off whatever they'd done the night before. By the time Mira and I descended the narrow, damp-stone steps into the market district, the underground bazaar was hitting its stride.

It was a strange place. Built into the vaulted cellars of a structure that had once been something official   a courthouse, maybe, or a guild hall   the market was a series of interconnected chambers with arched ceilings and stone pillars, lit by a combination of oil lamps, alchemical glow-jars, and the occasional shaft of sunlight that found its way down through grates in the street above. Stalls crowded every available space, their wares spilling into the walkways: dried herbs hanging in bundles from overhead beams, bins of minerals and reagents, racks of secondhand weapons, bolts of cloth in colors that suggested the dyer had access to compounds not available through conventional channels.

The clientele was equally varied. Mercenaries checking blades at a weapons dealer. A woman with elaborate tattoos on her forearms haggling for something in a dark glass bottle. Two men in hooded cloaks having a very quiet conversation over a table covered in small leather pouches. A group of children   street kids, by the look of them   running between stalls with the practiced ease of pickpockets in training, though one of them appeared to be genuinely shopping for turnips, which was either endearing or the best cover story I'd ever seen.

<<dialog "Mira">>
This way. The apothecary row is in the back chambers.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/day_2/3.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You know your way around down here.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I've been operating out of Valdmere for six years, on and off. You learn the geography. Especially the parts of the geography that sell things you need at prices the guild vendors won't match.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Is that a polite way of saying this is the black market?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's an efficient way of saying that licensed apothecaries charge three silver for a healing draught that costs them six coppers to make, and down here, the markup is only half that outrageous. Come on.
<</dialog>>

The apothecary stall was tucked into a corner of the back chamber, wedged between a candlemaker and a stall selling animal bones arranged in patterns that suggested either divination or interior decoration. The proprietor was a hunched old man with a face like a walnut   brown, deeply creased, and presenting the general impression of something that had been left in the sun too long. He sat on a stool behind a narrow counter covered in glass bottles, each one labeled in a cramped, spidery hand.
<<set $day_2_5 = false>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Buy healing draughts and bandages for the mine job">>
[[Enter the apothecary stall|Undercity]]

:: day_2_5
<<set $day_2_5 = true>>
<<dialog "Mira">>
Healing draughts. Two. Standard grade   pain reduction, anti-bleeding, anti-inflammatory.
<</dialog>>

The old man looked at her. Then at me. Then back at her. His eyes were sharp behind the wrinkles   the eyes of a man who'd been selling to sellswords for decades and could calculate the desperation coefficient of any customer within three seconds of eye contact.

<<dialog "Apothecary">>
Three silver each. Six total.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/day_2/4.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Mira">>
Five for the pair and you throw in an extra dram of numbing concentrate.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Apothecary">>
Five and a half. No extras.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Five. Or I walk to Kellam's stall on the second row and buy his, which are the same formula in uglier bottles for half a silver less.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Apothecary">>
Kellam's draughts are brewed with creek water and wishful thinking. They'll stop your bleeding the way a prayer stops a sword   technically possible, practically useless.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Then you should be confident enough in your product to match his price and keep the customer who knows the difference. Five silver. Two draughts. Final offer.
<</dialog>>

The old man's face compressed into an expression that had been seen on the faces of merchants dealing with half-elves since the dawn of commerce. The look of a man who knew he was going to lose and was trying to calculate how to make the loss feel like a victory.

<<dialog "Apothecary">>
Five silver. Two draughts and you tell people where you got them.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Done.
<</dialog>>

She counted out five silver coins and swept the two vials off the counter in a single motion, tucking them into the padded belt pouches she'd designated for exactly this purpose. The draughts were small   each one a finger-length glass tube sealed with wax, containing a liquid the color of diluted blood. Standard healing fare. They wouldn't regrow a limb or mend a broken bone, but they'd slow bleeding, reduce swelling, and dull pain for a few hours. Enough to keep a wounded sellsword moving until proper treatment was available. Good enough for goblin work.

We also bought a small tin of wound salve   one silver   and an extra roll of clean bandages for a few coppers. The salve smelled like pine resin and something bitter that Mira identified as tannin extract.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You're good at that. The haggling.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Half-elf. We're raised bilingual   Common and Negotiation.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I thought half-elves were raised bilingual in Common and Elven.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
That too. But Negotiation is the one that pays rent.
<</dialog>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Buy healing draughts and bandages for the mine job">>
[[Leave the apothecary row|day_2_5_1]]

:: day_2_5_1

We were leaving the apothecary row when Mira stopped. Her body did that thing   the sudden stillness, the locked attention, the shift from walking to targeting that happened between one step and the next. I followed her gaze.

An enchanter's stall. Small, tasteful, the wares displayed on black velvet like jewelry and on the velvet, glinting under the lamplight: arrowheads. Three of them, laid in a row, their edges catching the light with a faint orange shimmer that wasn't just reflection. Fire enchantments. Minor ones   they wouldn't turn an arrow into a fireball, but they'd heat the tip on impact, cauterizing the wound they made, making every hit significantly nastier. Goblin-killers, in the right hands.

The enchanter   a young woman with braided hair and steady eyes   noticed Mira's attention.

<<dialog "Enchanter">>
Fire-touched broadheads. Minor enchantment, guaranteed for twenty impacts or six months, whichever comes first. Five silver for three.
<</dialog>>

Mira looked at the arrowheads. I watched her look at them. Her fingers twitched at her sides   the unconscious reaching motion of an archer who's spotted the tool she needs and can't have. Her jaw tightened. Her amber eyes went flat in the way that meant she was suppressing something   desire, frustration, the particular pain of being good at what you do and unable to afford the equipment to do it better.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/5.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Five silver. We'd just spent five on healing draughts and another silver and change on salve and bandages. We had eight silver left between us. We needed those for the room, for food, for the hundred small expenses that kept two people alive in a city that charged for breathing.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Beautiful work. Maybe next time.
<</dialog>>

She walked away. Didn't look back. Her spine was straight, her stride even, her face composed. Nothing in her bearing betrayed the want I'd seen in her eyes for those three burning seconds.

I filed it away. The arrowheads, the five silver price, the stall's location in the back row of the Undercity market. If we came back from the mine with the cargo bonus, I'd buy them for her. I wouldn't say I was doing it. I wouldn't make a thing of it. I'd just leave them on her bowstring one morning and let her figure it out.

She'd know. She always knew. She'd punch me for being sentimental and then she'd use them and never miss a shot and that would be worth more than the money.
<<set $broken_antler_room = false>>
<<set $step = 2.5>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Go to Back to Broken Antler">>
<<link "Go to Back to Broken Antler">>
    <<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>


:: day_2_6
<<set $mcLocation = "broken_antler">>
<<set $broken_antler_room = true>>
The waiting was the worst part. We'd done the preparation. Gear checked, supplies bought, plan reviewed. Now there was nothing to do but sit in our room at the Broken Antler and wait for sunset, and waiting for sunset when you have a goblin mine to clear is the particular kind of torture that no enemy could improve upon, because the enemy is your own brain.

I tried to nap. Laid on the bed, closed my eyes, and instructed my mind to shut down for two hours. My mind responded by playing a detailed visualization of every possible way the mine job could go wrong, presented in vivid color with accompanying sound effects. Floor collapses. Ambushes from side tunnels. Getting lost in the dark. Running out of oil for the lantern. Goblin poison. Goblin traps. A goblin warband twice the size Grent estimated, waiting in the main chamber with weapons drawn and crude goblin smiles.

I opened my eyes after twenty minutes and stared at the water stain on the ceiling. Still shaped like a horse. The horse stared back with the implacable judgment of a plaster equine that had seen a lot of sellswords lie in this bed and worry about dying and then go out and either die or not die and either way the horse didn't care.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I can't sleep.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I know. You've been grinding your teeth for fifteen minutes. It sounds like a grain mill.
<</dialog>>

She was sitting by the window, her bow disassembled on her lap, performing the meditative ritual she did before every job. She'd removed the string   coiling it carefully, checking each inch for fraying or wear   and was now oiling the limbs with a soft cloth and a small pot of linseed oil. The motion was rhythmic, almost hypnotic. Long, slow strokes along the yew wood, following the grain, the oil darkening the surface and bringing out the warmth of the color. Her hands moved with the tenderness of a lover and the precision of a surgeon.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
How do you do that?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Oil a bow? You apply the oil and then you rub it in. It's not complicated.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/day_2/6.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Not the bow. The calm. How are you calm right now? We're about to crawl into a goblin hole in the dark and fight things that want to eat us, and you're sitting there oiling your bow like it's a pleasant afternoon at a spa.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Because the bow needs oiling and because being calm is more useful than being anxious. My hands are steady. My breathing is even. My heart rate is resting pace. In approximately five hours, all three of those things will be necessary to keep us alive. If I spend those five hours panicking, my hands shake, my breathing gets ragged, and my heart rate is already elevated before the first goblin shows its face. Panic is a resource you spend before you need it and then can't access when you do.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's very logical.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm a very logical person.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You once threw a boot at a stable hand because he looked at your horse wrong.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
He was going to feed him oats without checking for mold. That's not illogical, that's equine welfare. Different category entirely.
<</dialog>>

I watched her work. The bow was beautiful   not decoratively, not with filigree or carving, but with the simple beauty of a tool at the peak of its function. Yew wood, laminated with strips of horn on the belly and sinew on the back, the composite construction that gave half-elf bows their legendary draw weight without the bulk of a full longbow. She'd had it for six years, she'd once told me. Longer than any relationship, longer than any home, longer than any friendship except the one with the bow itself.

She checked each arrow next. Pulled them from the quivers one by one, rolling them between her fingers to check for straightness, squinting along the shaft for warps, testing the fletching   three feathers per arrow, goose, trimmed to identical shapes   for looseness or damage. Four failed the inspection. She set them aside with the resigned efficiency of a general cutting troops who weren't battle-ready.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Four bad arrows. That leaves twenty.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Twenty is enough. I don't plan to miss.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Nobody plans to miss.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Nobody else is me.
<</dialog>>

She said it without arrogance. Just fact. The same way she might say "the sky is up" or "water is wet." She was the best archer I'd ever seen   and I'd seen a few, including a full elf in Kestwick who could thread a needle at a hundred paces. Mira was better. Not by much, not with the supernatural grace of pure elven blood, but with something more dangerous: practice. Thousands and thousands of hours of practice, the obsessive repetition of a woman who'd spent her entire adult life perfecting one skill until it was less a skill and more an extension of her nervous system.

Twenty arrows. Twenty chances to kill something before it killed us. In Mira's hands, that was almost overkill. Almost.

I got up and went to the window, standing behind her. The market district was visible below   foot traffic thinner than usual in the post-rain dampness, merchants adjusting their awnings, a cart loaded with barrels navigating the narrow street with the careful precision of a ship through a reef. Normal life. The kind of life that happened to people who hadn't chosen to fight things for money.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Tell me about the worst job you've done. Before me. Before us.
<</dialog>>

She paused the oiling. Looked up at me with those amber eyes, evaluating the question the way she evaluated everything   quickly, thoroughly, deciding what level of honesty the moment deserved.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Why?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Because I can't nap, and thinking about someone else's worst day is better than thinking about my own upcoming one.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Fair enough. Three years ago. Vaelith   my mother's settlement, near the elven border. A merchant hired me to escort a wagon of goods from Vaelith to a trading post in the northern hills. Didn't tell me the goods were stolen from a local lord. Didn't tell me the lord had sent trackers.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
How many trackers?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Six. All elven. Full-blooded. Do you know what it's like to be tracked by full elves in a forest?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
No.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's like being hunted by the trees themselves. They don't make sounds. They don't leave tracks. They move through the canopy like wind. I spent three days sleeping in a creek bed because it was the only place they couldn't approach without me hearing them. I ate raw fish and drank silty water and had a bow and twelve arrows and the absolute certainty that I was going to die.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
But you didn't die.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I killed two of them on the second night. Ambush at a stream crossing. The other four decided the merchant's goods weren't worth losing more people and pulled back. The merchant was furious   said I should have killed all six. I told him that four full-blooded elven trackers deciding to leave you alone was the best outcome a half-elf could hope for and that he should consider it a professional miracle. Then I took my pay and left.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The worst part?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The worst part was that I recognized one of the two I killed. He'd been a friend of my mother's. I'd sat at his table when I was a child and eaten his wife's cooking. He probably didn't recognize me   I was eight the last time he saw me   but I recognized him. I recognized the way he fell.
<</dialog>>

She went back to oiling the bow. The motion was the same   steady, rhythmic, careful   but something in her hands had changed. A tightness. A deliberation. The hands of a woman who'd learned that the things you do to survive follow you home and sit at your table and eat your sleep.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm sorry.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Don't be. It's the job. We do what the job requires and we live with the residue. Tonight we're going to kill goblins. Tomorrow we'll live with it and the day after, we'll find another job and do it again. That's the life.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's the life.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Now stop asking me emotional questions and let me finish oiling this bow. We leave in four hours and I'd like to spend at least one of those not talking about my feelings.
<</dialog>>

I put my hand on her shoulder. Squeezed once. She didn't look up, but she leaned into the pressure   a fraction of an inch, barely perceptible, the Mira equivalent of a full embrace. The archer's version of holding hands.

I let go. Went back to the bed. Laid down. Stared at the horse stain.

Four hours to sunset. Four hours of waiting, of nerves, of the slow ratcheting tension that preceded every dangerous job. Four hours of being alive in a body I took for granted   the broad shoulders, the thick chest, the big hands that could wrap around a sword hilt or a woman's waist with equal confidence. Four hours of being exactly who I'd always been.

The last four hours of that kind of certainty, though I didn't know it yet.
<<set $day_2_7 = false>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Eat lunch at the Broken Antler">>
[[Lunch Time|broken_antler]]

:: day_2_7
<<set $day_2_7 = true>>
We ate lunch in the common room. A proper hot meal this time   we'd earned it, or at least Mira had decided we had, and when Mira decided something, the universe tended to cooperate rather than argue.

The innkeeper's stew was the kind of food that existed on the border between sustenance and punishment   gray meat in brown liquid with root vegetables that had been boiled past the point of species identification. But it was hot and it was filling and it came with bread that was only one day stale instead of two, which by Broken Antler standards qualified as artisanal.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/7.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

The common room was quiet at noon. A few day-drinkers at the bar, their backs curved into identical question marks of existential resignation. The innkeeper sweeping the floor with the enthusiasm of a man being paid exactly enough to move a broom but not enough to care where the dirt went. The orange cat asleep on the bar, one paw hanging over the edge, its tail twitching in some feline dream. 

Mira ate methodically. Fork, chew, swallow, repeat. Fueling up the way a soldier fuels up   treating the body as equipment to be maintained, calories as ammunition to be loaded. She ate everything on the plate and half of what was on mine, which I'd been pushing around with my spoon in the manner of a man who knew he should eat but whose stomach was conducting a vigorous protest march.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You're not eating.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm not hungry.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You weren't hungry at breakfast either. If you go into that mine on an empty stomach, your energy drops in the first fifteen minutes and you start making mistakes. The kind of mistakes that get people perforated.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Perforated. Nice word for it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm a nice person. Eat the stew.
<</dialog>>

I ate the stew. It tasted like anxiety served in a bowl.

We sat in our corner booth   the same one, always the same one, high-backed seats, wall at our backs, sightlines to both exits. The common room murmured around us. Someone was talking to the innkeeper about a leak in their room. A dock worker was staring into his ale with the thousand-yard stare of a man contemplating his life choices.

Mira reached across the table and took my hand.

The motion was small, almost casual. Her fingers wrapping around mine, the calloused pads of her bowstring-hand pressing against my calloused sword-hand palm. A brief squeeze. Contact that lasted maybe three seconds before she let go and went back to her stew as if nothing had happened.

My calloused hand. Her calloused hand. They fit together in a way that shouldn't have worked but did   rough against rough, scar against scar, two hands built for violence finding a moment of something gentle between them.

She didn't look at me when she did it. She didn't need to. The touch said everything her mouth wouldn't.

Be careful tonight. Come back. I'm not done with you yet.
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Wait for sunset and prepare for the mine job">>
[[Kill the time|day_2_8]]

:: day_2_8

I went down to the bar an hour later for something to settle my stomach. The ale was terrible but familiar, and sometimes familiar was all you needed.

Lenna was there.

Not working   it was daytime, her off-hours. She was sitting at the bar with a cup of tea and a piece of bread with honey, dressed differently than last night. Simpler. A linen blouse, loose, buttoned to the collar. Her chestnut hair pulled back in a practical knot instead of falling loose around her shoulders. No bodice, no presentation. Just a woman eating bread and drinking tea and looking younger in the daylight than she had by candlelight.

More real. That was the word. Last night she'd been wearing her profession like a costume. Now she was wearing herself.

She saw me and smiled. Not the professional smile   the warm, inviting, here's-what-I'm-offering smile from last night. A different one. Simpler. The smile of a woman who recognized someone she'd been briefly intimate with and didn't regret it.
