Back in the room, the afternoon heat was thick and close. The shutters were open but the air wasn't moving   the post-rain stillness that settled over Valdmere like a blanket, humid and heavy, making the room feel smaller than it was.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/9.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I'd gone down the hall to wash up at the basin   the single shared facility for this floor, a cracked ceramic bowl on a wooden stand beside a pitcher of water that was optimistically described as "fresh." I'd stripped to the waist, sluiced water over my head and neck and chest, scrubbed the road grime from my skin with a rag that smelled marginally cleaner than I did. The cold water felt good   sharp, clarifying, cutting through the pre-job fog that had been settling over my brain all morning.

I came back into the room toweling off my hair, shirtless, my trousers low on my hips, water trailing down my chest through the dark hair and Mira was on the bed.

She was sitting on the edge, checking her boot laces   one of her perpetual tasks, the constant maintenance of equipment that kept her alive   but her hands had stopped moving. She was watching me. Those amber eyes, half-lidded, tracking across my body with the focused attention she usually reserved for targets at distance. Shoulders. Chest. The trail of hair running down my stomach. The muscles of my arms as I dried my hair.

