<<updateobjective "day_2" "Leave the Broken Antler and head to the mine">>
[[Leave the City|day_2_9]]

:: day_2_9

Two hours after sunset. The sky was the color of old bruises   deep purple, streaked with gray, the clouds still thick but emptied of rain. The air was cool and wet and smelled like everything the rain had touched and released: stone, earth, rotting leaves, the distant salt of the harbor.

We left the Broken Antler armed and armored. My longsword on my hip, the weight of it familiar and reassuring, the scabbard tapping against my thigh with each step. Leather armor cinched tight over a linen tunic, the metal studs catching what little light there was. Mira's bow across her back, her quivers at her hips, the short sword   the cheese one   belted at her left side where she could draw it with a cross-body reach.

The common room barely noticed our departure. Two armed mercenaries heading out at night was unremarkable in Valdmere   the city's economy ran on questionable decisions made after dark.

The east gate guards barely glanced at us. One of them muttered something about the road being washed out past the second milestone. I didn't catch the details. I was already thinking about the mine.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/10.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Once we cleared the gate, Mira took point. Her half-elf eyes reading the darkness, picking out the obstacles I couldn't see   roots, stones, the treacherous spots where the rain-soaked loam had turned to mud over limestone. She communicated in hand signals, the system we'd developed over three months: closed fist for stop, two fingers for movement ahead, flat palm for get low.

I followed. Keeping my longsword sheathed to free both hands for balance. The ground was treacherous   mud over rock, a recipe for broken ankles and bruised pride. The creek Grent had mentioned was running hard, swollen with two days of downpour, the water churning over stones with a sound like grinding teeth.

[[Follow the Creek|day_2_9_1]]

:: day_2_9_1

Twice I stepped on what I thought was solid ground and sank knee-deep into mud. The second time, my boot stayed behind   a terrifying moment of sucking, pulling, grasping before I fished it out of the muck with a sound like a giant kissing a swamp.

Mira, of course, moved over the same ground like she was walking on marble. Half-elves.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/11.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Aldric">>
How are you not sinking?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Lighter. Lower center of gravity and I'm reading the ground surface, not just stepping on it. The darker patches are saturated   step on the lighter spots, where the water has drained.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's easy for someone with eyes that can see in the dark.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I don't see in the dark. I see in low light. There's a difference and stop talking. Sound carries over water.
<</dialog>>

I stopped talking. We followed the creek upstream. The moon was a sliver behind the clouds   good for stealth, bad for not dying in a mud pit. The forest pressed in from both sides, dripping, dark, the trees rising like pillars in a cathedral built by something that hated visitors.

We smelled the mine before we saw it.

<<link "Go to the Mine">>
    <<worldmap>>
<</link>>

:: day_2_10
<img src="img/scene/day_2/12.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

If you've never been downwind of a goblin camp, consider yourself blessed by whatever gods you pray to. It's not one bad smell. It's a symphony of bad smells, each one competing for dominance like drunks fighting for the last stool at the bar. Rotting meat. Unwashed bodies   not the honest road-sweat stink of humans, but the sharp, acrid reek of creatures who'd never encountered the concept of bathing and would eat the soap if given the chance. Urine. Feces and over all of it, the sour chemical tang of the fungal paste goblins smoked in their pipes, which smelled like burning hair soaked in vinegar.

It hit us from thirty paces. Mira wrinkled her nose   which for her, with enhanced half-elf senses, must have been like getting slapped across the face with a dead fish wrapped in a dirty diaper.

Fist up. Stop.

I stopped. She pointed ahead and to the right. Through the scrub, I made out the mine entrance   a ragged hole in the escarpment, framed by rotting timbers that sagged inward like the ribs of a dead animal. Eight feet wide, six feet tall. What had once been a proper entrance structure had collapsed into rubble, leaving a wound in the hillside.

A goblin sat on a flat rock outside. Wrapped in a human-sized cloak   stolen, probably from one of the dead caravan guards   that pooled around it like a collapsed tent. A crude spear leaned against the rock beside it. Head down. Chin on chest.

Asleep. The most pathetic sentry in the history of organized defense.

I drew a finger across my throat. Kill it? Mira shook her head. Practical reasoning   if it squeaked before dying, the sound would funnel into the mine like a megaphone. She held up one finger, pointed past the entrance. Go around.

We crept wide. Generous berth, careful steps over loose rock. My heart was loud in my chest   not fear exactly, but the heightened state that preceded every job, the animal awareness that sharpened everything. The crunch of gravel under my boots. The drip of water from the escarpment. The goblin's wheezing snores.

We slipped past. Ducked into the mine.
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Enter the mine and find the chemical storage shaft">>
[[Go into the Mine|day_2_11]]

:: day_2_11
<img src="img/scene/day_2/13.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />
<<set $mcLocation = "upper_tunnels">>
The air inside was different. Warmer. Heavier. It pressed against my face like a damp cloth, thick with wet stone and old iron and the goblin stench amplified by enclosure. The tunnel was wider than expected   room for two abreast, ceiling high enough that I only ducked for the sagging support beams. Walls of rough-hewn stone, dark iron ore veins glinting dully in the faint orange glow from deeper in.

Firelight. The camp.

Mira nocked an arrow. The whisper of bowstring drawing back, the click of nock against string. She moved forward, arrow ready.

I drew my longsword. In the tunnel it felt longer than usual, the blade nearly touching walls on either side at guard position. I'd have to fight tight. Short arcs. Jabs. Point work. Economy of motion in a space that left no room for anything else.

We moved.

The mine branched at forty paces. Main shaft ahead   firelight, sleeping goblins, a chorus of guttural snoring that sounded like a barnyard having nightmares. Side tunnel left   narrower, darker, cooler air carrying that sharp chemical smell.

Goblin markings scratched into the stone at the junction. I couldn't read goblin script but one symbol was clear enough: a rectangle with lines radiating outward. A box. Storage.

I pointed. Mira nodded. We took the side tunnel.

[[Go to the Storage Shaft|day_2_12]]

:: day_2_12
<img src="img/scene/day_2/14.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />
<<set $mcLocation = "storage_shaft">>
The deeper shaft was a different animal. Floor sloping steeply down, slick with moisture and fungal film   each step felt like walking on wet sponges. Walls narrowing until my shoulders nearly brushed stone on both sides. Mira's bow scraped the ceiling and she hissed a curse   the first sound either of us had made in ten minutes, echoing off the close walls like a shout.

Freeze. Listen. Nothing. Snoring continued above. Undisturbed. We kept moving.

The crates were twenty paces further down. Stacked against the tunnel wall behind a curtain of old mining canvas   heavy waxed fabric draped over them like a tablecloth. The Consortium seal stamped into the wood: scales over ship's wheel.

I counted by touch. Eight crates. Fingertips finding nail-heads on sealed lids. Four sealed. Two open but contents intact   glass vials nestled in straw packing, cool and smooth. Two gutted, straw scattered, vials smashed. The chemical smell was strongest here, leaking from broken containers.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Six recoverable. Four sealed, two open with contents.
<</dialog>>

I whispered so quietly the words barely had shape. Just breath with consonants.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Two each per trip. Three trips minimum.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Two. I'll rig carry harnesses from the canvas.
<</dialog>>

I reached for the mining canvas.

Mira's hand clamped on my forearm. Iron-hard. Her fingers digging into the muscle with strength that always surprised me. In the near-dark, her eyes caught the faintest light and reflected it back   two pale amber discs, wide and alert. Her head tilted, listening with ears that could hear a mouse fart at sixty paces.

Then I heard it too.

Footsteps. Purposeful. Coordinated. Multiple feet in formation, coming from the main shaft above.

A horn blew.

The sound was indescribable   a high, wailing blast that filled the tunnels like water filling a bottle, bouncing off every surface, doubling and tripling. Crude instrument, carved animal horn, but in the enclosed space it was deafening. My ears rang. My teeth vibrated. The stone walls hummed with it.

The sentry. Awake. A scent on the wind, a footprint in the mud, some goblin sixth sense. It knew.

The horn died. In the silence: voices. Goblin common   fast, harsh, consonants crashing into each other. Tone is universal. This tone said: Intruders. Find them. Kill them.

<<dialog "Mira">>
They know.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
They might not know where 
<</dialog>>

Second horn blast. Closer. Much closer. From the junction above us. They knew exactly where.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Move.
<</dialog>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Fight or flee?">>
[[Fight or Flight|day_2_13]]

:: day_2_13

I've made a lot of bad decisions in my life. The ale at the Broken Antler. The caravan job in Thornwall. The dwarf arm-wrestling incident in Kestwick. But I'd learned one thing from all of them: when the choice is between going deeper into the dark and fighting toward the light, you fight toward the light.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Back through the main shaft. We punch out.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
There's going to be a lot of them between us and the exit.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Then we go through them.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/day_2/15.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Mira slung her bow and drew the short sword. She held it the way she always did   like it owed her money and she was about to collect.

We ran.

The slope was treacherous going up   the same fungal slime that made it unpleasant descending now turned it into a scramble, boots sliding on wet stone. My free hand bracing against the wall, sword hand keeping the blade forward. Mira behind me, breathing controlled and steady, the professional calm of someone who intended to survive this.

We hit the junction at a run. Turned hard into the main shaft.

Straight into five goblins.

They were ugly. Knee-high, yellowish-green skin stretched tight over knobby bones, teeth jutting from their jaws at angles suggesting their dentist had been a rock slide. Small cunning eyes gleaming with the particular malice of creatures who survived by being mean and fast. Crude iron cleavers. Wooden shields reinforced with scavenged metal.

No time to think. Body before brain   training, instinct, ten years of muscle memory compressed into one explosive motion. My longsword came up in a tight arc that caught the first across the face. Wet crack. It dropped like a puppet with cut strings.

The second went low   classic goblin tactic, hamstring the tall-folk, let gravity and blood loss do the rest. It ducked under my blade and lunged for my knees. I stomped its weapon arm, felt the bones snap under my boot heel, and drove the sword through its back before it could scream.

Behind me, Mira's bow sang   she'd switched at the last moment, the confined range be damned. At ten paces in a tunnel, her arrows didn't arc. They punched. The third goblin took one through the eye socket and rocketed backward into the fourth, both going down in a shrieking tangle.

The fifth turned to run. Its alarm screech   rusty hinge being tortured   bounced off every surface, echoing and re-echoing.

So much for quiet.

I stepped over the bodies. Blood   goblin blood, thin and almost black, smelling like old copper and sour milk   spattered my armor, my arms, my face. I wiped it from my eyes and pressed forward.

The main shaft ahead. Firelight casting jumping shadows and there   the quality of the light changing from orange to blue-gray where moonlight filtered in. The entrance. Forty paces. Fifty. Close enough to taste.

More goblins poured from side tunnels. Six from the left, stumbling over each other. Three from an alcove I'd missed. Two dropped from a natural shelf above me   a ledge I'd walked under without looking up, because I was an idiot and goblins were not.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/16.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

The tunnel became a meat grinder.

I fought the way I'd been taught   control the center line, make them come to me. In a tunnel this narrow, they approached two abreast at most, which meant my longsword's reach was an advantage. I choked up on the grip, shortened my swings, used the blade like an oversized dagger. Jabs. Short slashes. Pommel strikes when they got too close. A goblin tried to climb over its dead companion and I caught it with a rising cut that opened its belly. It fell screaming, tangling the ones behind it.

Mira was at my back. The short sword   the weapon she hated   doing the ugly close work she was terrifyingly good at. I could hear her behind me: the grunt of effort, the meaty impact of steel on flesh, creative obscenities directed at her weapon. An arrow whizzed past my ear   she'd switched between bow and sword in a heartbeat, loosing a shot over my shoulder that took a goblin in the throat, then drawing the blade again for the next one.

Thirty paces from the entrance. Twenty-five. Twenty. The floor gave out.
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Fall into the pit">>
<<changestat "health"   -15>>
