[[Crawl to Freedom|day_2_15]]

:: day_2_15

She got under my arm, her shoulder against my ribs, and heaved me up the side of the pit. Half-elf strength   leaner, denser muscle, pound for pound significantly more powerful than human. She carried most of my weight without complaint, though the strain showed in the cords of her neck and the set of her jaw.

We made the top. The entrance   fifteen paces. Moonlight. The smell of rain-washed air. Freedom.

Five more goblins between us and the way out.

I looked at Mira. She looked at me.

<<dialog "Mira">>You can't fight.<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>Watch me.<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>You can barely stand.<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>I don't need to stand. I need to swing.<</dialog>>

She propped me against the tunnel wall. My back to the stone, ruined leg stretched out. Longsword in both hands, blade angled across my body. I couldn't advance, retreat, or maneuver. All I could do was hold the wall and kill anything within reach.

Mira picked up her bow. Nocked an arrow. Stepped in front of me.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Stay behind me and try not to die. I'm not carrying a corpse back to Valdmere.
<</dialog>>

What followed was the longest two minutes of my life.

She fought like something from a battle song. Two arrows into the first goblin before it closed to ten paces   both in the chest, the creature staggering back and falling. She dropped the bow, drew the short sword, and met the next two at the tunnel's narrowest point, using the walls to prevent flanking.

The cheese sword moved in tight, efficient arcs. Parry, riposte, a lateral cut that opened a goblin's arm to the bone. It dropped its weapon. She finished it through the neck.

One got past her.

It came from my left, low and fast, cleaver raised. I was sitting against a wall with a destroyed ankle and a perforated calf and a sword that felt like it weighed a thousand pounds. The goblin was small and quick and smiling with all forty-seven of its horrible teeth.

I swung.

Not elegant. Not trained. A desperate sweeping cut driven by adrenaline and the absolute refusal to die in a goblin mine sitting in filthy water. The blade caught the creature across the midsection and kept going, powered by terror, cutting deep enough that I felt the edge scrape against its spine.

The goblin fell in two directions at once.

One left. The big one. Broader than the rest, thick, crude metal armor hammered onto its shoulders, a helmet made from what looked like a human skull with the top sawn off. A leader. A sergeant. The kind others followed because it was meaner and uglier and harder to kill.

It came straight at me. Not at Mira   at me. The injured prey. The one who couldn't run. Predator logic.

Mira put an arrow in its shoulder. It didn't slow down.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/19.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I brought my longsword up. The goblin sergeant's blade   a proper short sword, looted from the caravan   came in hard and fast, diagonal slash aimed at my neck. I parried. The impact rattled my arms to the shoulders. This thing was strong   not human-strong, but wiry, dense, with the cornered-animal power that compensated for size.

Our blades locked. It leaned in, pressing weight against my sword, trying to slide its edge to my hands. Its breath hit my face   warm, rancid, raw meat and pipe fungus.

I headbutted it.

My forehead cracked against the bridge of its nose   goblins had surprisingly solid noses, dense cartilage over thin bone   and it staggered back, green-black blood spurting from its nostrils. Not much room. But enough.

I thrust. The longsword went in under the crude armor, between the ribs, and found something vital. The goblin sergeant made a sound   quiet, almost surprised, like someone told unexpected news   and folded forward over my blade.

Twist. Withdraw. It fell.

Silence. The tunnel quiet except for my ragged breathing and the drip of water and blood.

Mira stood over me. Short sword dripping. Chest heaving. She looked down the tunnel toward the camp. Nothing coming.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Can you move?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Not well.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Good enough.
<</dialog>>

She hauled me up. One arm over her shoulder, most of my weight on her, my ruined leg dragging. We limped to the entrance. Past the sentry's rock   empty now, the alarm-blower long gone. Into the rain-washed air of the forest.

I'd never loved the smell of rain more in my entire life.
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Escape the mine and get back to Valdmere (Cristen District) to find a healer">>
<<changestat "health"   -20>>
<<changestat "energy"    -20>>
[[Go Outside|day_2_16]]

:: day_2_16
<<set $mcLocation = "mine_entrance">>
<img src="img/scene/day_2/20.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Fifty paces out. I told her to stop. The pain in my ankle had settled into a constant throbbing heat spreading up my leg. The calf wound was bleeding again   the makeshift bandage she'd tied from a strip of her own sleeve was soaked through.

She lowered me against a tree trunk. Pulled my boot off   both of us working it, her pulling, me biting a leather strap, and when it came free the relief and agony arrived simultaneously. My ankle was twice its normal size. Deep purple-blue, visible even by moonlight. She probed with her fingers and I gripped tree roots hard enough to leave nail marks.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Not broken. Badly sprained. Maybe a torn ligament. The calf is worse.
<</dialog>>

She unwrapped the bandage. The splinter wound was ugly   a ragged puncture in the meat of the muscle, deep enough that the edges gaped. Something still in there. I could feel it   foreign body, rotten timber fragment, sitting inside my calf like a sliver of malice.

<<dialog "Mira">>
There's still wood in the wound. I can't get it out here. Not in the dark, not with dirty hands. If it stays in, it'll fester.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Then we find a healer.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
In Valdmere? At this hour? The temple healers won't see you without a donation we can't afford, and the apothecary on Mill Street closes at sunset.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
There has to be someone.
<</dialog>>

The forest dripped around us. Patient. Indifferent.

<<dialog "Mira">>
There's someone. In the Undercity. A woman I've heard about. She operates out of the old cistern district near the Lowgate.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You've HEARD about her.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
From other sellswords. People who get hurt at inconvenient hours and can't afford temple prices.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What kind of healer operates out of a cistern at midnight?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The kind who doesn't ask questions and doesn't charge much. Her name is Ysolde. They call her the Cistern Witch.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's not reassuring.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
No one calls her that to her face. Listen to me.
<</dialog>>

She crouched in front of me, her amber eyes catching the moonlight.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You've got a piece of rotten wood buried in your calf. If we wait until morning, you'll have an infection. A bad one. The kind that takes legs. I've seen it happen twice. First man lost the leg at the knee. Second man lost it at the hip, and then lost the rest of himself three days later.
<</dialog>>

I looked at my leg. The swelling getting worse. The wound edges hot   unnaturally hot.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Fine. The Cistern Witch.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Ysolde.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Whatever. Take me there.
<</dialog>>
<<set $day_2_17 = false>>
<<link "Get Back to Valdmere, Cistern district">>
    <<worldmap>>
<</link>>

:: day_2_17
<<set $day_2_17 = true>>
Getting back to Valdmere took three times as long as getting out. Mira half-carried me through the dark forest   my arm over her shoulder, her arm around my waist, both of us stumbling like the world's worst three-legged race. My leg throbbed with every step. The bleeding left a trail of dark drops on wet leaves behind us.

We entered through the south gate. River side. Fewer guards. Cheaper bribes. Mira dropped a copper into the night guard's hand without breaking stride. He looked away with the practiced disinterest of a man who'd seen worse.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/21.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

The Undercity was different at night than during the morning's market run. Not safer, not more dangerous, just different   the way a forest is different at night, the same geography rearranged by shadow and silence. The narrow streets felt like throats. The leaning buildings like conspirators. Water seeped up through cobblestone cracks.

The cistern district was the lowest point of the Undercity. The old stone cisterns   the city's ancient water supply, drained and sealed centuries ago   sat under sagging buildings that shifted as the ground slowly settled. The streets here were perpetually damp. The air tasted of mineral and mold.

Mira stopped in front of a door.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/22.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Unremarkable. Wooden. Weathered. Set into stone between a shuttered chandler's shop and a building that smelled of fermenting something. No sign. No marking. Just a door.
<<set $day_2_18 = false>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Find Ysolde, the Cistern Witch, and get her to treat your leg">>
[[Knock on the door|CisternDistrict]]

:: day_2_18
<<set $day_2_18 = true>>
She knocked. Three times, then twice, then once. Silence. Then footsteps   soft, measured, unhurried. A bolt. A chain. The door opened inward.

The woman standing in the doorway was not what I expected. I'd been picturing a cistern witch. Hunched. Warted. The kind who'd have a black cat and a cauldron and would cackle at inappropriate moments. Ysolde was none of those things.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/23.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

She was tall   nearly my height   and thin, with the angular, austere beauty of a woman who'd been striking in youth and had hardened into something more interesting with age. Fifty, maybe older. Iron-gray hair pulled back in a severe braid over one shoulder. Eyes so dark that in the lamplight, iris disappeared into pupil. They moved over me with the quick assessing gaze of someone who saw injured bodies so regularly the diagnosis was happening before I opened my mouth.

Simple linen dress under a leather apron stained with things I didn't want to identify. Hands   long-fingered, steady, surprisingly elegant   hanging at her sides and on her left wrist, a bracelet of small glass vials, each containing a different colored liquid. They clinked softly when she moved, like tiny wind chimes.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
The leg.
<</dialog>>

Not a question.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Splinter wound. Deep. There's still wood in the muscle and the ankle   sprained, possibly torn.
<</dialog>>

Ysolde looked at Mira, then at me. Those dark depthless eyes evaluated, calculated, and arrived at a conclusion in the space of a heartbeat.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Bring him in.
<</dialog>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Ysolde has agreed to treat your leg. Go inside her workshop.">>
[[Go inside|day_2_19]]

:: day_2_19
<<changestat "health"   10>>
<<changestat "energy"   10>> 
The inside of Ysolde's practice   workshop   lair   was a single large room that had once been a cellar. Stone walls. Low ceiling. The faint sound of water running somewhere beneath the floor. Lit by oil lamps and beeswax candles, the light warm and steady.

Shelves lined every wall, packed with glass bottles, ceramic jars, bundles of dried herbs, rolled bandages, instruments ranging from medical to obscure. A heavy wooden table in the center, scarred and stained with decades of use. A smaller table beside it covered in a white cloth, arranged with tools   forceps, needles, thread, scissors, and a small bone saw that I very much hoped was decorative.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/24.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

The smell was better than expected. Herbs   lavender, sage, something sharp and clean that cut through the damp. Professional.

Mira helped me onto the central table. Cold wood against my back through damp clothes. Ysolde moved around the room gathering materials   basin of water, strips of clean linen, a leather case that unrolled to reveal delicate surgical instruments.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Trousers.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Remove them. Or I'll cut them off. Your choice.
<</dialog>>

Mira helped me peel the trousers down over my swollen ankle. The process involved enough suppressed screaming to power a small theatrical production. The calf wound was worse than it had looked in the forest   angry red skin, swollen edges, yellowish seepage from the deepest part.

Ysolde examined it with her fingers. Firm but not rough. Practiced hands. She pressed around the wound, probing, and I gripped the table edges.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Deep. Approximately two inches of foreign material still embedded. The wood has begun to splinter inside the wound track. This will be unpleasant.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
More unpleasant than getting it was?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Possibly.
<</dialog>>

She selected three bottles from the shelf. Clear. Amber and a deep, unsettling green. She poured the clear liquid over the wound first   clean antiseptic burn that made me hiss through my teeth. Then she opened the leather case and selected long, thin forceps.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Hold still and try not to scream. My neighbors are already irritated with me.
<</dialog>>

The extraction took ten minutes. It felt like ten hours.

She worked with meticulous precision, forceps probing the wound track, finding fragments of rotten timber buried in my calf muscle and withdrawing them one by one. Eight pieces. From matchstick-size to a splinter the length of my little finger that came out trailing a dark streamer of blood.

I held still. I did not scream. I may have whispered words that would have gotten me banned from every temple in the city, but I did not scream.

When the last fragment came out, she irrigated again with the clear liquid, then applied the amber solution   warm, viscous, with a numbing quality that spread through the wounded tissue like a blessing. The pain didn't disappear. It retreated. Screaming foreground to dull distant throb.

She stitched the wound closed. Twelve neat sutures, each placed with the care of a seamstress on a wedding dress. Wrapped it in clean linen, firm but not tight.

The ankle got a different treatment. She manipulated the joint   testing range of motion, pressing specific points that made me wince and one that nearly launched me off the table. Then a poultice   green, foul-smelling   packed around the swollen joint and wrapped in place.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Severe sprain. Partial ligament tear. Two to three weeks if you stay off it. If you insist on walking, wrap tightly and use a staff.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The calf?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
The infection risk is the concern. The wood was rotten. Old mine timber. Full of microbial contamination.
<</dialog>>

She turned to the shelf and selected a small glass vial. The green one. The liquid inside was dark, almost black-green, and seemed to shift when she tilted it. Something inside was moving. Or seemed to be.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Drink this.
<</dialog>>

I looked at the vial. It looked back.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What is it?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
A restorative compound. Anti-infectious, anti-inflammatory, promotes tissue regeneration. My own formulation.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What's in it?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Things you wouldn't recognize if I named them.
<</dialog>>

She held it out. Patient. Unhurried.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
You can refuse, of course and you can walk out of here with a wound that will fester within twenty-four hours. At which point you'll need a temple healer   one who will charge you ten times what I'm charging and will probably still take the leg.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/day_2/25.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I looked at Mira. She shrugged   a gesture that communicated both I don't know and what choice do you have?

I took the vial. Uncorked it. The smell was strange   sharp and green, like fresh-cut herbs, but underneath that, something metallic. Electric. Like the air before a lightning storm.

I drank it.

Bitter first. Then sweet. Then warmth   spreading from my throat down into my chest like swallowing liquid sunlight. Not bad. Just a lot. A lot of sensation, a lot of something moving through me, mapping me from the inside.

Ysolde watched me drink. Her expression was unreadable. Not concern. Not satisfaction. Something patient. Something evaluating. Like a gardener watching a seed she'd just planted, already knowing what it would become but willing to wait for the soil to do its work.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Good. The effects will begin within twenty-four hours. You'll feel warmth, possibly tingling. That's normal. Your body is fighting the infection.
<</dialog>>

She took the empty vial back. Placed it on her shelf.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Eight coppers.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Eight coppers? For all of that?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
For the extraction, the suturing, the poultice, and the compound. I told you I don't charge much.
<</dialog>>

I paid her. Most of my remaining coin. After the precision, the care, the numbness making my leg feel almost human   it felt like a bargain.

Mira helped me off the table. My ankle protested but held, with Mira's shoulder to lean on. We moved toward the door.

I paused at the threshold. Something nagging at me. Something about the way Ysolde had watched me drink. The patience. The attention.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The compound. You said the effects begin within twenty-four hours.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
That's correct.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What effects, exactly? Beyond fighting the infection?
<</dialog>>

She looked at me with those dark, depthless eyes and she smiled   small, closed-lipped, an expression that didn't quite reach her eyes and didn't quite answer the question.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Healing. The body knows what it needs. The compound simply... facilitates.
<</dialog>>

I didn't like that answer. I didn't like the smile. But my leg hurt, and I was tired, and the numbness from the amber solution was making everything feel soft and distant, and Mira was already steering me toward the door.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Rest tonight. Sleep well and come back if you need anything.
<</dialog>>

The door closed behind us. The cistern district swallowed us back into its damp, dark streets. My leg throbbed dully under the bandages, the numbness already beginning to wear off at the edges.

But underneath the pain, something else. A warmth in my core that had nothing to do with the amber solution. Deeper. Subtler. Like the vial's contents were reaching into parts of me that had nothing to do with the wound in my calf, touching something fundamental and rearranging it, quietly, without permission.

I shook it off. Exhaustion. Blood loss. A long night and a longer walk home. Nothing more.
<<set $day_2_20 = false>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Rest for the night and let the compound work.">>
<<link "Return to the Broken Antler">>
    <<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>

:: day_2_20
<<set $day_2_20 = true>>

<img src="img/scene/day_2/26.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

We made it back to the Broken Antler just before dawn. The eastern sky was going gray-pink over the rooftops. Bakers' smoke. Cart wheels. The distant clang of the harbor bell. The city waking up, ordinary people beginning ordinary days.

The tavern door was unlocked   it always was. The innkeeper didn't look up from his ledger as Mira hauled me through the common room and up the stairs. Whatever he thought of two blood-spattered mercenaries limping through his establishment at dawn, he kept it to himself. That was the kind of tavern the Broken Antler was.

She helped me onto the bed. My bed. The lumpy one with the mattress that was more regret than cushion. It was the most comfortable thing I'd ever felt.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We didn't get the crates.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We didn't die. That's worth more than the crates.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Grent's going to want his advance back.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Grent can find us. We'll deal with it tomorrow.
<</dialog>>

She pulled my boots off   the intact one and the one she'd already removed in the forest. Covered me with the blanket. The wool scratched against my skin and the sensation was sharper than it should have been   more textured, more detailed, like the nerve endings in my arms had been polished to a higher sensitivity.

I noticed it. Filed it away. Didn't think about it.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Sleep. I'll watch for a while.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You don't need to 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Sleep, Aldric.
<</dialog>>
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Sleep">>
[[Sleep|day_2_21]]

:: day_2_21
/* <img src="img/locations/valdmere/broken_antler_room.jpeg" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
I slept. My last thought before the darkness took me was about the vial. The way the liquid had looked   alive, almost. Shifting inside the glass like something breathing and Ysolde's smile when I'd asked about the effects. That patient, knowing smile. The smile of a gardener watching a seed.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/dream/1.jpeg" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

Healing. The body knows what it needs. Then the dark swallowed me whole.
<<updateobjective "day_2" "Wake Up">>
[[Am I Drowning?|day_2_22]]

:: day_2_22

Normally, I am not dreaming at least, not like this. Not the kind of dream where you know you're dreaming but can't wake up, where the impossible is just part of the scenery and you accept it without question. I don't have those kinds of dreams. But this one   this was different.

Water. I was underwater. Not drowning   breathing, somehow, the way you can only breathe in dreams, where the impossibility of it never occurs to you. The water was warm. Body-warm. The temperature of blood, of bathwater left to cool just past comfort, and it pressed against me from every direction like hands   hundreds of hands, soft and insistent, touching everything at once.

My skin was wrong. 

That was the first thing. Not pain, not cold, just wrong   the water touching me and the nerves underneath responding in a language I didn't speak. Too sensitive. Every current, every ripple registered against my body like fingertips tracing lines, and the sensation was so sharp it bordered on unbearable. I could feel the water moving across my chest and the feeling was   more. Heavier. Like there was more of me there to feel it, more surface area catching the current, more nerve endings firing where before there had only been flat hard muscle and coarse hair.

I looked down.

The water was dark   not black, but the deep bruised blue of a midnight ocean   and I couldn't see clearly. Just shapes. My own body distorted through the medium, wavering like a reflection in troubled glass. But the shapes were wrong. The shoulders were narrower than mine. The waist cinched inward like something had taken a sculptor's hand to my torso and carved away the sides and below the waist   hips. Wide, round, flaring outward in a curve that no amount of underwater distortion could explain. My hips had never curved like that. My hips were straight, narrow, a man's hips built for holding up a sword belt and nothing else.

These hips were built for something different entirely.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/27.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I tried to touch them. My hands moved through the warm water   slow, dreamlike, fighting the resistance   and when they found the curve of those hips, the fingers that pressed into soft skin were slender. Elegant. The calluses gone, the knuckles smooth, the nails clean and shaped in a way mine never were. A woman's hands. Small enough that the hips they rested on felt wide and full beneath them, the flesh giving under gentle pressure the way a man's body never gives.

I pulled the hands away. Watched them drift in front of my face. Long delicate fingers, tapered, the tendons visible under skin that was pale and unblemished and soft. I flexed them. They responded. They were mine. They couldn't be mine.

Something brushed my shoulders. Hair. Long hair, drifting in the current like dark seaweed, floating across my vision in lazy tendrils. I reached up and caught a strand between those wrong fingers. It was dark brown   my color   but three feet long, thick and glossy and heavy with water, and when I tugged it I felt the pull at my own scalp, felt the roots anchored in the crown of my own head.

The water pulsed. Warmer. The hands were back   not mine, the invisible ones, the water-hands   and they moved across my body with purpose now. Across my stomach, which was flat but soft, layered with a thin feminine padding that had replaced the ridged muscles I'd spent years building. Up over my ribs, which felt smaller, closer together, the cage of bone contracted like something had pressed it inward from both sides and then higher.

Breasts.

The water-hands cupped them and I gasped   or tried to, the water filling my open mouth with warmth instead of air   because the sensation was blinding. Not pain. Pleasure. The sudden, shocking, unwanted pleasure of sensitive flesh being held, lifted, the weight of them settling into invisible palms. They were full. Heavy. Soft in a way that muscle never is, yielding and warm, and the nipples   gods, the nipples   hardened against the current like they'd been touched by ice, and the sensation of that tightening traveled from my chest straight down through my belly and into a place between my legs that felt nothing like what was supposed to be there.

I cried out. The sound that came from my throat wasn't my voice.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/dream/3.jpeg" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

It was higher. Breathier. A woman's gasp, musical and startled, echoing through the dark water like a bell struck underwater. I clapped those slender hands over my mouth and felt lips that were fuller than mine, softer, the lower one plump and trembling against my fingertips.

The water began to spin. Slowly at first, then faster, a warm vortex pulling me downward into darker depths, and the invisible hands were everywhere now   stroking my thighs (thick, soft, pressing together), tracing the new curve of my waist, sliding up the arch of my back which bent differently, moved differently, had a flexibility and a sway that my spine had never known. 

Every touch sent sensation cascading through me in patterns I couldn't map   pleasure radiating from places that had no business being pleasurable, aching in places that had no business aching, and underneath all of it a low, liquid heat pooling between those pressed-together thighs, building in an organ I didn't have, couldn't have, shouldn't have. The water swallowed me.

[[Go to the surface|day_2_23]]

:: day_2_23

I broke the surface. Not water anymore. Air. I was standing   when had I started standing?   in a room. Stone walls. Candlelight. The smell of lavender and something sharper underneath, metallic, electric. Ysolde's workshop. But larger. The walls stretched away into shadow, the ceiling too high to see, the candles floating in the air without holders, their flames burning green and ahead of me, a mirror.

Not the cheap polished tin at the Broken Antler. This was a proper mirror   tall, full-length, framed in dark wood carved with symbols I didn't recognize. It stood in the center of the room on nothing, just hovering there, its surface rippling like the water I'd just escaped.

<img src="img/scene/day_2/dream/4.jpeg" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I looked into it. Me. Thank the gods, me. Square jaw, dark stubble, deep-set green eyes under heavy brows. The scar through my left eyebrow   the knife fight in Redhaven, age twenty-one. My face. My shoulders, broad and thick. My chest, flat and hairy. My body, exactly as it had always been, familiar as my own name.

I exhaled. Stepped closer. The reflection didn't step with me.

It stood still. Watching me approach with my own green eyes and as I stared, the face in the mirror... shifted. Like watching ice melt in fast motion, the hard edges softening, blurring, reforming. The jaw narrowed. The angles rounded. The stubble thinned to nothing, the skin beneath it going smooth and pale. The cheekbones lifted, sharpened, became prominent ridges that caught the green candlelight and threw delicate shadows across a face that was still mine and already wasn't.

My lips   the reflection's lips   grew fuller. Pinker. The lower lip swelling into a soft pout, the upper lip developing a pronounced cupid's bow that I'd never had, could never have had. The brow ridge flattened, the forehead smoothing into a gentle curve, and the eyes   still green, still mine   appeared larger, framed by lashes that were thick and dark and long enough to cast shadows on cheeks that were softer than any cheek I'd ever owned.

"No," I said. The reflection said nothing. Its mouth   her mouth, the lips that were ripe and trembling   curved into a smile I recognized. My smile. The crooked one that pulled to the right. Except on this face, that familiar asymmetry was devastating. It wasn't a man's wry smirk anymore. It was a woman's knowing grin, and it hit me in the chest like a fist.

The mirror flickered. Now the reflection was further along. The neck was slender, the throat smooth   no Adam's apple, not even a shadow of one. Collarbones stood out in elegant lines below shoulders that were narrow, delicate, nothing like the broad slabs of muscle I carried and below the collarbones   breasts. Real, full, unmistakable breasts filling the reflection's chest, round and soft with large pink nipples and the particular gentle sway of natural weight. The reflection breathed and they rose and fell, and I watched myself   watched her   breathe in a body I didn't own.

The waist was impossible. A deep, dramatic curve inward, a waist I could span with my two hands   my real hands, the large calloused ones   creating an hourglass against the wide flare of hips that were built for a purpose my hips had never served. The belly was flat but soft, a gentle feminine curve below the navel, and the hips... the hips were generous, lush, the kind of hips that moved when they walked, that swayed with every step, that drew the eye down along curves that didn't end so much as flow into thick, soft thighs.

