The dark dissolved. I was standing in a street. Valdmere. The market district, near the east gate   I recognized the cobblestones, the awnings, the fishmonger's stall with its eternal reek. But the light was wrong. Golden, amber, the color of late afternoon through honey, and everything had that soft-edged dream quality where details were sharp in the center of vision and smeared to nothing at the periphery.

People moved around me. Market crowd. Merchants, housewives, street vendors, beggars. Nobody looked at me. I was invisible, a ghost in my own city.

